  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "type": "text",
      "paragraphs": [
        "Each item belongs to a category whose GL class (FEED, FNB, STORES) decides the inventory account (1300, 1310, 1320) and the expense account of issues and adjustments. Transfers between locations change no value, so they post nothing.",
        "The movement ledger is append-only: a mistake is corrected by a new movement, never by editing an old one. A database guard refuses any movement that would make stock negative.",
        "Purchasing books goods receipts here through the receipts API, and builds requisitions from the reorder report."
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inventory-erd",
      "title": "Inventory Control database architecture · Visual ERD",
      "type": "text",
      "intro": "<strong>5 domain tables, 7 declared foreign keys.</strong> Items, locations, lots and movements are kept separate so the system can explain what stock exists, where it moved, and why.",
      "paragraphs": [
        "<figure class=\"tc-inventory-erd\"><a href=\"/img/inventory-erd.svg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" aria-label=\"Open full-size Inventory ERD\"><img src=\"/img/inventory-erd.png\" alt=\"Inventory Control ERD: item category, inventory item, stock location, stock lot and stock movement; seven foreign keys including the enterprise core venue\" loading=\"lazy\"></a><figcaption>Physical MySQL 8 inventory schema, built from <code>10_inventory.sql</code>. Arrows indicate declared foreign keys; the venue is an external Enterprise Core reference.</figcaption><div class=\"tc-inventory-actions\"><a class=\"btn btn-primary btn-sm\" target=\"_blank\" href=\"/img/inventory-erd.svg\">Open full-size ERD</a><a class=\"btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm\" download href=\"/img/inventory-erd.svg\">Download SVG</a><a class=\"btn btn-outline-primary btn-sm\" download href=\"/img/inventory-erd.png\">Download PNG</a><a class=\"btn btn-outline-secondary btn-sm\" href=\"/schemas/inventory\">Database schema details</a></div></figure>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inventory-plain-english",
      "title": "Inventory explained in everyday business terms",
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Business question",
        "Database table",
        "Simple explanation"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "What kinds of supplies do we hold?",
          "<code>item_category</code>",
          "Groups items such as feed, bedding, food, beverages and stores."
        ],
        [
          "What exactly do we stock?",
          "<code>inventory_item</code>",
          "The SKU, unit of measure, standard cost and reorder point are defined once per item."
        ],
        [
          "Where is the stock?",
          "<code>stock_location</code>",
          "A named stock area belongs to a venue shared with Enterprise Core."
        ],
        [
          "Which purchase or batch did this come from?",
          "<code>stock_lot</code>",
          "Lot number, optional expiry date and unit cost preserve traceability."
        ],
        [
          "Why did stock change?",
          "<code>stock_movement</code>",
          "Receipts, issues, transfers, adjustments and returns are recorded as movements with quantities and source references."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inventory-relationships",
      "title": "Seven foreign keys: business meaning",
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Child field → parent key",
        "Why it matters"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "<code>inventory_item.category_id → item_category.id</code>",
          "Every inventory item belongs to a valid category."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_location.venue_id → enterprise_core.venue.id</code>",
          "Stock locations inherit the club’s shared venue identity."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_lot.item_id → inventory_item.id</code>",
          "Every lot belongs to a real inventory item."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_movement.item_id → inventory_item.id</code>",
          "Every recorded movement concerns a known item."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_movement.lot_id → stock_lot.id</code>",
          "A movement can optionally be traced to a particular lot."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_movement.from_location_id → stock_location.id</code>",
          "If a source location is supplied, it must exist."
        ],
        [
          "<code>stock_movement.to_location_id → stock_location.id</code>",
          "If a destination is supplied, it must exist."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inventory-lifecycle",
      "title": "From purchase receipt to the General Ledger",
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Step",
        "Business action",
        "What the ERP records"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "1. Define",
          "Register oats with SKU, units, category, cost and reorder point.",
          "<code>inventory_item</code> links to <code>item_category</code>."
        ],
        [
          "2. Receive",
          "Purchasing receives oats against a purchase order.",
          "Inventory records a <code>RECEIPT</code> movement into a location, optionally with a lot."
        ],
        [
          "3. Store / transfer",
          "Move stock from feed barn to another location.",
          "A <code>TRANSFER</code> records both source and destination; a transfer within the same valuation scope may not require a GL event."
        ],
        [
          "4. Consume",
          "Issue oats for stable operations.",
          "An <code>ISSUE</code> movement decreases stock; the service emits the configured accounting event."
        ],
        [
          "5. Reorder",
          "Available stock falls below the configured reorder point.",
          "A report can tell Purchasing what to replenish; reorder decisions require service logic."
        ],
        [
          "6. Reconcile",
          "Compare physical counts and financial stock valuation.",
          "Adjustments and their audited source references support reconciliation to GL inventory accounts."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "inventory-controls",
      "title": "What the database guarantees — and what the application must enforce",
      "type": "table",
      "columns": [
        "Constraint or rule",
        "Enforced by SQL?",
        "Architectural takeaway"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "<code>quantity &gt; 0</code> and <code>unit_cost &gt;= 0</code>",
          "Yes — CHECK constraints",
          "Movements record positive quantities, while direction comes from movement type and locations."
        ],
        [
          "At least one source or destination location",
          "Yes — CHECK constraint",
          "The row cannot have both locations empty."
        ],
        [
          "Source and destination differ when both exist",
          "Yes — CHECK constraint",
          "Prevents a transfer from a location to itself."
        ],
        [
          "Existing item, lot, and locations",
          "Yes — foreign keys",
          "All non-null references must point to rows that exist."
        ],
        [
          "Lot belongs to the same item as the movement",
          "Not guaranteed by these FKs",
          "Validate <code>stock_movement.item_id</code> against <code>stock_lot.item_id</code> in business logic."
        ],
        [
          "No negative stock after issues or transfers",
          "Not established by this DDL",
          "Requires atomic balance checks and appropriate concurrency control in the application or triggers."
        ],
        [
          "Movement ledger is immutable",
          "Not established by this DDL",
          "Treat append-only behaviour as an application/permission design and verify implementation."
        ],
        [
          "Balanced GL event exactly once",
          "Not established by this DDL",
          "Use transactionally recorded events, idempotent posting and GL reconciliation."
        ]
      ]
    },
